[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62871,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Extragastrointestinal stromal tumour (EGIST) is a mesenchymal neoplasm with variable behaviour, characterized by differentiation towards the interstitial cells of Cajal, but occurring outside the gastrointestinal tract.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62872,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8936/3 Gastrointestinal stromal tumour</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62873,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F74 &amp; XH8RP6 Neoplasms of uncertain behaviour of peritoneum &amp; Gastrointestinal stromal tumour NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62874,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62875,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62876,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EGISTs mainly affect the omentum, mesentery, and retroperitoneum {19440146; 28288036}. They may represent metastasis from an unrecognized primary or a detached mass from the gastrointestinal tract. Exceptionally, EGISTs can involve solid organs, such as the liver and pancreas.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62877,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients commonly present with an abdominopelvic mass, abdominal discomfort, and/or pain. Asymptomatic small tumours are detected incidentally during surgery or radiological examination.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62878,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EGISTs are rare and may occur at any age.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62879,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62880,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most EGISTs harbour gain-of-function mutations of the <em>KIT</em> or <em>PDGFRA</em> oncogene.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62881,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "<p>EGISTs can be solitary or multiple, and they may be of considerable size. The cut section can be solid or cystic with haemorrhage and necrosis.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-19T16:02:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62882,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>The histopathological features of EGISTs are similar to those of their counterpart in the gastrointestinal tract. Solitary tumours are similar to gastric gastrointestinal stromal tumours (GISTs), whereas multinodular EGISTs are more likely to show histological features similar to those of small intestinal GISTs. Some cases have indeterminate histology {19440146; 28288036}. The histological patterns include spindled, epithelioid, vacuolated, nested, and myxoid {19440146; 28288036}.</p>\r\n<p>Immunophenotypically, &gt;&nbsp;90% of EGISTs are positive for KIT (CD117). DOG1 antibody may rescue diagnostically as many as 50% of KIT-negative GISTs {15215166; 18223323; 19606013; 20716168}. Most spindle cell GISTs are positive for CD34, whereas epithelioid examples are less consistently positive.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-19T16:03:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62883,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62884,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Approximately 70% of EGISTs harbour mutations of the <em>KIT</em> or <em>PDGFRA</em> gene {19440146; 28288036}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62885,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Essential:</em> circumscribed morphologically uniform spindle cell or epithelioid tumour with KIT (CD117) and/or DOG1 immunopositivity.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-19T16:03:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62886,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Risk stratification is preferred to anatomical staging {29846513}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62887,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Inoperable EGISTs have a worse prognosis {28288036}. Tumours with small intestinal GIST features are more likely to have an aggressive course {10824931; 19440146}. Brisk mitotic activity and necrosis are also adverse prognostic factors {10824931; 19440146; 28288036; 29599305}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]